# SYNOPSIS

```ndb
match=pci vendor=0x1af4 device=0x1052 program=/boot/bin/drv-virtio-input
    post=input# msi=1
```

# DESCRIPTION

`drv-virtio-input` drives a virtio input device and serves it on the input class protocol (docs/proto/input.md) to inputd(8), on `/srv/input0`, `/srv/input1`, … in the order devmgr(8) finds them.

What it is comes from the device's configuration: absolute axes make a tablet, relative ones a mouse, and anything else with keys a keyboard; a pointer with a wheel says so.
The device speaks Linux's evdev; the driver turns a keyboard's key codes into HID usages, each key record carrying the held set after it, and a pointer's report, ended by `SYN_REPORT`, into one pointer record.
Records go to inputd a batch per interrupt, never merged.
virtio-input gives no timestamps: a record's time is when the driver saw it.

It serves one session at a time; with none, what the device reports is dropped.

# SEE ALSO

inputd(8), driver(6), devmgr(8)

# BUGS

No LEDs: the status queue is not used.
QEMU sends a tablet's buttons to its mouse, when there is one.
